  "overview": {
    "text": "Reykjavík is Iceland’s capital and most populous municipality, on the country’s southwest coast. Its name means “Smoky Bay,” referring to steam rising from nearby hot springs; geothermal water is also used to heat homes and swimming pools. The city is linked to Iceland’s earliest settlement: archaeological remains in its centre date to around 870. The traditional story that Ingólfur Arnarson chose the site by following pillars cast into the sea is a legend, not a confirmed account. Reykjavík’s urban growth began in the 18th century, and it received town rights in 1786. Government offices moved there in 1904, when it became Iceland’s capital and an important national centre.",
